questions.questionText:
Consider the following fragment of C++ code:



const int x = 8;

const int y = 16;

if(x > y)

{

    int z = x + y;

}



For codes like the above one, which technique of code optimization is appropriate?

What is the output of the following C++ fragment code?

int a = 6, b = 8;
int x = 2, y = 4;
int c = (x > y ? (a--, x) : (b--, y));
cout << "a=" << a;
cout << "    b=" << b;
cout << "    c=" << c;

What is the output of the following Java fragment code?

int[] list = new int[4];
int sum = 0;
for(int i = 0; i < list.length; i++)
{
    list[i] = i^3;
    sum += list[i];
}
System.out.print(sum);

What is the output of the following fragment C++ code?
for(int n = 1; n <= 18; n = n + 2)
{
    if(n % 7 != 0)
    cout << n << " ";
    else
    break;
}

What is the output of the following Java fragment code?

int n = 6, m = 15, p = 3;

n -= m;

n *= p;

System.out.print(n);

What is the output of the following C++ fragment code?
int a, b;
a = 13;
b = 9;
while ((20 && 0) && (a > b))
{
    cout<<"Plants are our life";
}
